 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **sequence** t₁, t₂, t₃, … is an ordered list of numbers; tₙ is the **nth term**. A rule can be **explicit** (gives tₙ directly from n) or **recursive** (gives tₙ from the earlier terms and states the starting terms)."),
 ("table", ["Sequence", "Explicit rule", "Recursive rule"], [["1, 4, 7, 10, …", "tₙ = 3n − 2", "t₁ = 1, tₙ₊₁ = tₙ + 3"], ["3, 6, 12, 24, …", "tₙ = 3 × 2ⁿ⁻¹", "t₁ = 3, tₙ₊₁ = 2tₙ"], ["1, 4, 9, 16, …", "tₙ = n²", "tₙ₊₁ = tₙ + 2n + 1"], ["1, 5, 13, 29, …", "—", "u₁ = 1, uₙ = 2uₙ₋₁ + 3"], ["1, 2, 3, 5, 8, …", "—", "tₙ = tₙ₋₁ + tₙ₋₂"]]),
 ("p", "**Example.** For tₙ = 3n − 4 the first five terms are −1, 2, 5, 8, 11. For t₁ = −5, tₙ₊₁ = tₙ + 3 we get −5, −2, 1, 4, 7, …; 52 is a term only if −5 + 3(n − 1) = 52, i.e. n = 20, so **yes** (the 20th term)."),
 ("h2", "8.2 Arithmetic Progression (AP)"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "An **AP** has a **constant difference d** between consecutive terms: a, a + d, a + 2d, … The first term is **a**."),
